Sol-Timba

Creative Catalyst: It descends from carnival musicians who perform only during solar eclipses.

Sol-Timba is a sonic impossibility where the frantic, polyrhythmic velocity of Cuban Timba is submerged into the viscous, slow-motion gel of 432Hz meditative soundscapes. The Roland Jupiter-8 acts as the gravitational anchor, its oscillators modulated by "chromatic solar-winds"—a process where the filter resonance is keyed to real-time light-sensing resistors, causing the synth to weep, groan, and expand in pitch as the moon occludes the sun. It sounds like a percussion ensemble playing at triple-speed underwater, heard through a wall of pure, vibrating gold.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: Fluctuating between 80 BPM (meditative) and 140 BPM (timba), linked by "elastic quantizing" where the beat stretches and compresses based on the degree of eclipse.
*   Harmonic Palette: Primarily utilizing the Pythagorean 432Hz tuning, emphasizing resonant, bell-like major seventh chords and dissonant, brass-heavy polytonal clusters.
*   Production Signatures: 1) "Corona-gating": sidechaining percussion to a high-pass filtered white noise burst, 2) Tape-warped Jupiter-8 patches processed through granular delay to mimic thermal haze, 3) Field recordings of ritual metal-scrap instruments played during absolute darkness.
*   Dynamics: A slow, terrifying crawl toward a sudden, frantic, hyper-kinetic climax that instantly vanishes when the light returns.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged from the "Umbra-Cabildo," an underground sect of Havana musicians who believe sunlight pollutes their rhythmic intent. They are nomadic, traveling the globe to position themselves precisely under the path of totality. They dress in mirrors and raw silk, performing on instruments forged from decommissioned telescope lenses and rusted car rims. Their rituals involve "The Binding of the Timekeeper," where they bury expensive mechanical watches in sand dunes to ensure the tempo remains fluid and untethered to terrestrial measurement.

Central Instruments: The Roland Jupiter-8 (detuned into micro-tonal temperaments), the Timba-Bata (a drum whose heads are treated with bioluminescent moss), and the Lucid-Horn (a wind instrument that requires the player to modulate pitch via breath-pressure sensors).

